WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:03.100
What about the Q&A session?

00:00:03.150 --> 00:00:05.300 
Where should we put it in the presentation?

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:08.200 
It’s scary just to think about it

00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:11.300 
I don’t know how to handle questions

00:00:12.200 --> 00:00:14.030
Don’t worry

00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:16.250 
If you follow some simple guidelines

00:00:16.260 --> 00:00:19.030 
you should have no problem at all

00:00:19.100 --> 00:00:21.260 
First, you should let your audience know 

00:00:21.270 --> 00:00:24.000
when they can ask questions

00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:26.160
You could even give the audience

00:00:26.170 --> 00:00:29.250 
some instructions for asking questions

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:33.140
Sometimes the audience is told to raise questions

00:00:33.150 --> 00:00:35.100
whenever they have one

00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:37.300
Yes, I’ve heard some speaker say 

00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:39.260
“Feel free to interrupt me 

00:00:39.270 --> 00:00:41.200
if there are any questions.”

00:00:42.050 --> 00:00:45.300 
But I prefer to leave the Q&A session until the end

00:00:46.160 --> 00:00:49.000
Then you could say, “I am happy to

00:00:49.010 --> 00:00:52.100 
answer any questions after the presentation” 

00:00:52.150 --> 00:00:56.200
or “I’ll leave ten minutes at the end for questions.”

00:00:57.100 --> 00:01:00.050
You can also invite your audience 

00:01:00.060 --> 00:01:02.000
to bring up questions

00:01:02.050 --> 00:01:04.120
“Are there any questions for me?” 

00:01:04.130 --> 00:01:08.230
or “I am glad to answer any questions now.”

00:01:08.860 --> 00:01:10.110
Sometimes I’m not sure 

00:01:10.120 --> 00:01:12.200
if they’re satisfied with my answers.

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:14.250 
How should I check again?

00:01:15.100 --> 00:01:18.120
It wouldn’t hurt to double check by saying

00:01:18.130 --> 00:01:20.220
"Does this answer your question?"

00:01:20.230 --> 00:01:24.100 
or "Do you follow what I am saying?"

00:01:24.200 --> 00:01:27.200
What if I don’t understand what they’re asking? 

00:01:28.100 --> 00:01:30.300
How can I ask them to clarify?

00:01:31.100 --> 00:01:33.100
You could say “I’m sorry 

00:01:33.110 --> 00:01:36.050
but I don’t quite understand your question

00:01:36.100 --> 00:01:37.300
Could you rephrase it?”

00:01:38.200 --> 00:01:41.250
I might not know the answer to their questions

00:01:42.200 --> 00:01:44.250 
How could I explain that?

00:01:45.100 --> 00:01:48.000
You can honestly let them know that

00:01:48.100 --> 00:01:52.000 
I am afraid I don’t have the answer at the moment

00:01:52.010 --> 00:01:55.020
I’ll check on it and get back to you later

00:01:55.030 --> 00:01:59.100 
or Good question. What do the rest of you think?

00:02:00.050 --> 00:02:02.100
And remember to thank your audience

00:02:02.110 --> 00:02:04.100 
for asking questions

00:02:04.250 --> 00:02:08.150 
That’s a good question. Thank you for bringing it up 

00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:12.080
or “I am glad you asked that question

00:02:12.100 --> 00:02:16.100
Usually the question session is followed by the closing

00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:18.190
How do I signal to the audience 

00:02:18.200 --> 00:02:21.300
that I won’t be able to take any more questions?

00:02:22.100 --> 00:02:24.150
Use a sentence like

00:02:24.160 --> 00:02:27.100 
Are there any other questions at this point?

00:02:27.110 --> 00:02:32.140 
or I think I have time for one last question

00:02:32.150 --> 00:02:36.200
or If there are no more questions, I’ll leave it here

00:02:37.100 --> 00:02:39.070
This is a hint to the audience 

00:02:39.080 --> 00:02:42.300
that the Q&A session is about to end

00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:46.160
Ok, if you don’t have any other questions

00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:48.500
it’s time for us to go

00:02:49.100 --> 00:02:52.000
Ok, I got the hint

00:02:52.020 --> 00:02:54.300
Thanks for the tips. Talk to you later
